---
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const modes = [
  { name: "RAIN", meaning: "Rain is the earliest meaningful precipitation." },
  { name: "SNOW", meaning: "Snow is the earliest meaningful precipitation." },
  { name: "MIX", meaning: "Mixed or freezing precipitation appears first." },
  { name: "TEMP", meaning: "No meaningful precipitation appears within nine hours." },
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="How to read the forecast — Burnished Sky Ahead"
  description="A visual guide to the three-node weather forecast in Burnished Sky Ahead."
  current="support"
>
  <div class="page-shell">
    <header class="page-intro forecast-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">Burnished Sky Ahead · Field guide</p>
      <h1>Three points.<br />The hours ahead.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The lower-left miniature is a timeline. Read each column from top to
        bottom: local clock hour, forecast node, then its value.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="forecast-examples" aria-labelledby="examples-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Two common readings</p>
        <h2 id="examples-title">Clock time, not a countdown.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="forecast-example-grid">
        <article class="forecast-example">
          <div class="forecast-example-heading">
            <span class="forecast-symbol rain" aria-hidden="true">◆</span>
            <div>
              <p class="forecast-mode">RAIN · 3H</p>
              <h3>Rain in the next three hours</h3>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="forecast-rail" aria-label="At 6, 7, and 8 o'clock, rain chances are 20, 60, and 80 percent">
            <div class="forecast-point">
              <span class="forecast-hour">06</span><i></i><strong>20%</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="forecast-point">
              <span class="forecast-hour">07</span><i></i><strong>60%</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="forecast-point">
              <span class="forecast-hour">08</span><i></i><strong>80%</strong>
            </div>
            <span class="forecast-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            If the current time is 05:32, these labels mean 06:00, 07:00, and
            08:00. The percentages belong to the node beside each hour.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="forecast-example temp-example">
          <div class="forecast-example-heading">
            <span class="forecast-symbol temp" aria-hidden="true">◇</span>
            <div>
              <p class="forecast-mode">TEMP · 6H</p>
              <h3>A dry six-hour temperature view</h3>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="forecast-rail" aria-label="At 7, 9, and 11 o'clock, forecast temperatures are 28, 27, and 26 degrees">
            <div class="forecast-point">
              <span class="forecast-hour">07</span><i></i><strong>28°</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="forecast-point">
              <span class="forecast-hour">09</span><i></i><strong>27°</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="forecast-point">
              <span class="forecast-hour">11</span><i></i><strong>26°</strong>
            </div>
            <span class="forecast-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            When the next nine hours are meaningfully dry, the module shows
            actual forecast temperatures at +2, +4, and +6 hours.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section content-grid">
      <aside>How it decides</aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The shortest useful horizon.</h2>
        <p>
          The face uses Garmin’s cached hourly forecast and selects the earliest
          meaningful precipitation within nine hours. It then chooses a 3H, 6H,
          or 9H view. A distant 9H view appears only when precipitation begins
          later—not as a default.
        </p>

        <div class="forecast-mode-grid">
          {modes.map((mode) => (
            <article>
              <strong>{mode.name}</strong>
              <p>{mode.meaning}</p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h2>What changes by horizon.</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>3H:</strong> each node represents the next individual hour.</li>
          <li><strong>6H:</strong> each precipitation node summarizes a two-hour window.</li>
          <li><strong>9H:</strong> each precipitation node summarizes a three-hour window.</li>
          <li><strong>TEMP 6H:</strong> the nodes show temperatures at +2, +4, and +6 hours.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For RAIN, SNOW, and MIX, a bucket displays the highest precipitation
          chance in its interval. The printed hour is the end of that interval.
          The watch follows your 12/24-hour setting and crosses midnight naturally.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            <strong>Missing stays missing.</strong> If Garmin has no trustworthy
            value or forecast time, Burnished Sky Ahead shows <code>--</code>
            rather than inventing a number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="action-row">
          <a class="button primary" href="/burnished-sky-ahead/">Back to the watch face</a>
          <a class="button" href="/burnished-sky-ahead/support/">Support</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
